1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,689
Vehicles, equipment, facilities: all of it runs on handovers.

2
00:00:04,909 --> 00:00:08,339
At every shift change, the detail that matters is lost.

3
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:14,685
So answers end up scattered across notes, calls and systems, and managers chase them by hand.

4
00:00:14,905 --> 00:00:17,819
Every chase costs hours that never reach the work.

5
00:00:18,039 --> 00:00:23,090
Meanwhile the critical issues stay buried until they cause delay, rework or harm.

6
00:00:23,810 --> 00:00:29,565
One break at handover, three costs: scattered answers, lost time, buried risk.

7
00:00:29,785 --> 00:00:34,985
The pattern runs across service sectors. We show it in one: automotive.

8
00:00:35,705 --> 00:00:39,037
Here is the solution, powered by Deloitte and NVIDIA.

9
00:00:39,757 --> 00:00:42,585
This is where the Service Operations Agent comes in.

10
00:00:42,805 --> 00:00:46,611
One live view of every job, shown in automotive service.

11
00:00:46,831 --> 00:00:50,678
Right now, 12 vehicles are flagged as unsafe to release.

12
00:00:51,698 --> 00:00:55,594
Here is how it works: a technician speaks or types an update.

13
00:00:55,814 --> 00:01:00,033
NVIDIA Riva transcribes it, and Nemotron extracts the facts.

14
00:01:00,253 --> 00:01:03,544
Code verifies every fact. Nothing is guessed.

15
00:01:03,764 --> 00:01:05,804
Each update joins an audit trail.

16
00:01:06,024 --> 00:01:09,175
Managers simply ask, and receive cited answers.

17
00:01:10,195 --> 00:01:13,861
Let us follow one update. A technician dictates it from the bay.

18
00:01:14,081 --> 00:01:16,719
NVIDIA Riva turns the speech into text.

19
00:01:16,939 --> 00:01:20,930
Nemotron extracts the facts: the measurement and the spec.

20
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:26,481
Code verifies it and flags the repair order unsafe to release, instantly.

21
00:01:27,801 --> 00:01:32,161
A manager asks: which vehicles cannot be released on safety grounds?

22
00:01:32,381 --> 00:01:37,252
13 vehicles, each one cited, in a tenth of a second.

23
00:01:37,872 --> 00:01:42,228
An open question: has anyone seen a whistling noise on a Passat?

24
00:01:42,448 --> 00:01:47,265
Search, reranking and a cited answer, in seconds, on one GPU.

25
00:01:48,485 --> 00:01:52,056
Business impact: unsafe vehicles surface immediately.

26
00:01:52,276 --> 00:01:54,731
Managers get answers in a tenth of a second.

27
00:01:54,951 --> 00:01:58,986
Every figure traces to a real record, and one update feeds it all.

28
00:01:59,706 --> 00:02:05,276
Behind it, the architecture: three NVIDIA NIMs on one L40S.

29
00:02:05,896 --> 00:02:10,350
Riva handles speech. NeMo Guardrails stops unsafe requests.

30
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:14,186
NeMo Evaluator scores every release: the whole stack.

31
00:02:15,406 --> 00:02:20,511
It is all observable: DCGM and app metrics, live in Grafana.

32
00:02:21,531 --> 00:02:27,000
In summary: safety first, with unsafe vehicles flagged as updates are logged.

33
00:02:27,220 --> 00:02:31,854
Answers in a tenth of a second, traced to records, on one GPU you own.

34
00:02:32,074 --> 00:02:37,457
Fast. Grounded. Yours to run. Shown in automotive, built for service operations.

